What Is "Max Execution" Locally?
It depends on your machine, but here’s a rule of thumb:
Machine Type	Safe Max Threads	Notes
🧑‍💻 Standard laptop (8 GB RAM, 4 cores)	50–100 threads	May lag with listeners enabled
💻 Developer desktop (16+ GB RAM, 8+ cores)	100–300 threads	Avoid complex post-processors
🖥️ Load testing server	500+ threads	Use non-GUI mode and distributed testing



✅ Key   Summary Report Metrics (Explained Simply)
Metric	          What It Means	                                          |What to Look For (Typical Benchmarks)
Label	            Name of the request or transaction	                    |Grouped logically (e.g., Login, Search, Logout)
# Samples	        Total requests sent for that label	                    |Consistency in counts expected for each request
Average	          Mean response time for the request (ms)                 |Login: < 1,500ms
                                                                          |Search: < 2,000ms
                                                                          |Logout: < 1,000ms
Min / Max	        Fastest / slowest recorded times	                      |Max should not spike > 5s unless expected (e.g., large searches)
Std. Dev.	        Response time variability	                              |Lower is better (< 10–20% of average is ideal)
Throughput	      Requests/sec processed by the server (incl. all users)	|Higher is better; reflects server's handling capacity
Received KB/sec	  Download rate per second	                              |For bandwidth usage insights (optional)
Errors %	        % of failed requests	                                  |Should be 0%, or < 1% max

✅ What is Throughput in JMeter?
Throughput = How many requests are processed per second (or minute)
🧃 Real-World Example:
Imagine you own a juice shop.
You can serve 5 customers per minute — that's your throughput.
In JMeter terms:
If your test sends 300 requests in 1 minute, your throughput = 5 requests/second (or 300/minute).


JMeter Report Descriptions
1.🧮 Aggregate Report
A tabular summary of all request metrics:
📊 Avg, Min, Max, Std. Dev, Throughput, Error %, etc.
Ideal for: Overall performance snapshot across all samplers.
2.🌲 View Results Tree
Shows individual request details (request, response, headers, etc.)
Useful for: Debugging, validating response content, and seeing failures.
3.📈 Graph Results
A live graph of response time, throughput, and error rate.
Great for: Visual trends during the test (not post-run focused).
4.📊 Summary Report
Similar to Aggregate Report but includes additional throughput & network metrics.
Good for: Comparing performance under different thread settings.
5.🧾 View Results in Table
Displays each individual request line-by-line in a table view.
Useful for: Checking timing and result code for each sample.
6.⏱ Transaction Per Second (TPS)
Graph showing how many transactions (requests) are executed per second.
Use this to evaluate: System load handling and request frequency.
7.📉 Response Time Over Time
Tracks how response time changes throughout the test duration.
Helps you spot: Performance degradation, slowdowns, or recovery trends.
8.👤 Active Threads Over Time
Shows how many users (threads) were active at each point in time.
Useful to verify: Ramp-up behavior and concurrent user load.
